Salicylate Intolerance
Integrative & Functional medicine
Pruritic hives/flushing following salicylate exposure, Severe sinus congestion/nasal polyps, Widespread migrating joint aching, Resolution on low-salicylate diet

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-033
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe systemic flare involving generalized intensely pruritic hives, profuse rhinorrhea, and severe wheezing following dietary ingestion, requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute life-threatening anaphylaxis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing rapid-onset facial flushing, full-body intensely itchy hives, severe nasal congestion, and acute airway hyperreactivity (wheezing) after eating. Urgent on-site physical and hemodynamic assessment was strictly required to exclude life-threatening IgE-mediated anaphylaxis with impending airway occlusion, preventing catastrophic respiratory collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My skin is on fire with hives, my nose is completely blocked, and my chest feels so tight I can't get a full breath in."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden, highly severe exacerbation of symptoms beginning 60 minutes ago, strictly following the consumption of highly specific distinct high-salicylate foods (a large portion of berries and almonds, washed down with mint tea). The patient rapidly developed sudden, distinct severe intensely pruritic generalized hives (urticaria) and prominent facial flushing. This was followed by recurrent, distinct severe intense sinus congestion and profuse rhinorrhea. The patient complains of an exacerbation of distinct severe asthma symptoms, experiencing distinctly increased profound severe airway hyperreactivity and tightness. Concurrently, the patient reports chronic, severe abdominal cramping, intense bloating, and distinctly altered bowel habits, alongside widespread vague migrating joint aching. The patient has a known history of severe nasal polyps. Historically, symptoms resolve entirely upon initiating a highly strict distinctly low-salicylate elimination diet.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, actively scratching, sitting upright to maximize respiratory effort.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of progression to severe respiratory failure if airway hyperreactivity worsens.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious, visibly flushed, actively scratching diffuse wheals, and is speaking in mildly truncated sentences.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals widespread, intensely erythematous, raised, and highly pruritic wheals (urticaria) across the trunk and upper extremities. Head and neck exam shows profound bilateral nasal mucosal edema with clear rhinorrhea, but absolutely no uvular or tongue angioedema (upper airway is open). Respiratory exam reveals mild, bilateral expiratory wheezing with slightly prolonged expiratory phase, but no silent chest or severe retractions. Cardiovascular exam confirms sinus tachycardia without hypotension. Abdominal exam shows mild distension and hyperactive bowel sounds. The presentation matches the diagnostic tetrad: pruritic hives/flushing following salicylate exposure, severe sinus congestion/nasal polyps, widespread migrating joint aching, and airway hyperreactivity (Samter's Triad variant). Confirmation relies on total resolution following the complete removal of dietary salicylates.
Environmental Observations: Patient has a known history of asthma; Salbutamol inhaler is present on the table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient used Salbutamol MDI (2 puffs) prior to arrival with partial relief of wheeze. No NSAID use reported today.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Salicylate Intolerance (Acute Flare/Pseudoanaphylaxis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute IgE-Mediated Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Maintenance of normal blood pressure, absence of upper airway angioedema (lips/tongue), and the specific known trigger profile (salicylates rather than classic IgE allergens like peanuts), though clinically the presentation mimics anaphylaxis and must be treated aggressively.

Acute Asthma Exacerbation (Viral induced) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The simultaneous, sudden onset of profuse generalized urticaria and severe GI cramping immediately following a specific dietary trigger.

Hereditary Angioedema - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of severe pruritic hives (HAE is typically non-pruritic and lacks urticaria) and the prominent asthmatic component.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe, multi-systemic hypersensitivity reaction featuring generalized urticaria, extreme sinus congestion, and active lower airway bronchospasm (wheezing). Telephone triage correctly identified the critical, time-sensitive need to evaluate for impending anaphylactic shock and total airway occlusion. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to directly visualize the oropharynx, auscultate the lungs, and assess oxygen saturation and blood pressure. The physical exclusion of upper airway angioedema and profound hypotension confirmed the patient was not in immediate, decompensated shock requiring IM Adrenaline. However, the severity of the generalized hives, the active bronchospasm (SpO2 95%), and the extreme physical distress dictated that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a distinct, unacceptable risk of progression to severe respiratory failure, hypoxia, and immense suffering. This urgent intervention unequivocally satisfies MBS Items 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Promethazine 25mg orally stat to aggressively block H1 receptors and reduce urticaria. Supervised the administration of 4 puffs of Salbutamol via spacer, resulting in clearance of the expiratory wheeze and SpO2 improvement to 98%. Advised strict avoidance of all high-salicylate foods and all NSAIDs (aspirin, ibuprofen). Recommend urgent GP follow-up for immunology referral and formal dietary planning.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the severe pseudo-allergic overflow, initiate strict dietary controls and proactive respiratory management. Action: Implement a rigid, immediate cessation of all berries, almonds, mint, spices, and NSAIDs. Consequence: Eliminating exogenous dietary and pharmacological salicylates physically prevents the inhibition of the COX-1 enzyme, explicitly preventing the leukotriene overproduction that triggers the severe hives and asthma flares. Action: Utilize the Salbutamol inhaler exactly 15 minutes prior to any necessary exercise or exposure to cold air over the next 48 hours. Consequence: Proactive bronchodilation stabilizes the hyper-reactive mast cells in the airways, directly rewarding the patient by preventing secondary asthmatic crashes while the systemic inflammation clears.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated currently.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours to reassess chest clearance and review allergy action plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if developing a sudden change in voice, visible swelling of the lips or tongue, feeling of the throat closing, severe worsening shortness of breath not relieved by Salbutamol, or feeling faint/collapsing.

